<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaiah was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> prophet active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the later 8th century BCE, during the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzziah">Uzziah</a>, Jotham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. He warned against reliance on foreign alliances and, according to the Bible, assured Hezekiah that Jerusalem would survive the Assyrian siege of 701 BCE. The Book of Isaiah, which includes later prophecies, is one of the most influential books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaiah.jpg" alt="Isaiah" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michelangelo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jesaja_(Michelangelo).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…population of the Kingdom of Israel, the &quot;Ten Lost Tribes&quot; of biblical tradition, profoundly marked the history of Israel and Judah; the Assyrian crisis is reflected in the prophetic books of <b>Isaiah</b>, Hosea and Micah, and in the biblical account of Sennacherib's siege of Jerusalem in 701 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Prophets such as <b>Isaiah</b>, Micah and Jeremiah criticised social injustice and religious practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…three parts: the Torah, the five books of Moses; the Nevi’im, the Prophets, which include the histories of Israel from Joshua to the kings and the books of prophets such as <b>Isaiah</b>, Jeremiah and Ezekiel; and the Ketuvim, the Writings, among them the Psalms, Proverbs, Job and Daniel.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Six Ge'ez inscriptions are known from 6th-century South Arabia, from the reign of Kaleb of Axum, the king who conquered Himyar; they record his conquests, name Jesus, Mary and David and quote <b>Isaiah</b>, the Psalms and Matthew.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oldest surviving manuscripts are among the Dead Sea Scrolls, found from 1947 near Qumran, such as the Great <b>Isaiah</b> Scroll, copied around 125 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings governed with councils of elders and the heads of great merchant families; the prophet <b>Isaiah</b> called Tyre the city “whose merchants are princes”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Judah. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.&#8221;</p><footer>Isaiah 2:4 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool.&#8221;</p><footer>Isaiah 1:18 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; the prophet speaking in the name of God &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Woe is me! for I am undone; because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people of unclean lips: for mine eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts.&#8221;</p><footer>Isaiah 6:5, his vision in the Temple &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them.&#8221;</p><footer>Isaiah 11:6 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Woe unto them that call evil good, and good evil; that put darkness for light, and light for darkness; that put bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter!&#8221;</p><footer>Isaiah 5:20 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
